Multi-search: several keys in one op=get
========================================

An onak extension to HKP. The HKP draft (draft-gallagher-openpgp-hkp) has no
way to ask for several certificates at once, so a client refreshing fifty of
them makes fifty requests. Here it makes one.

Request
-------

    GET /pks/lookup?op=get&search=0x<id>&search=0x<id>&...[&options=mr]

- Each search is a key ID (16 or 8 hex digits) or a fingerprint (40 or 64),
  written with its 0x. A text search among several is refused: it is the one
  search that can match thousands of keys.
- At most 100 searches. That is the largest round number whose request line
  fits under the 8 KB that Apache and nginx accept by default, at 74 bytes
  per v6 fingerprint.
- A single search is unchanged, text included.

Response
--------

- Every op=get answer carries the header

      X-HKP-Multi-Search: 100

  naming the limit. A client learns from its first get, of any kind, whether
  the server takes several searches, and how many.
- 200: one armored block holding every certificate found, each once, however
  many searches named it. Searches that found nothing are simply absent.
- 404: none of them found anything.
- 400: several searches, one of which is not a 0x key ID or fingerprint.
- 413: more searches than the limit.

For a client
------------

Without the header, the server does not know this extension. It has then
answered one of the searches only, the first or the last depending on the
implementation: ask for the others one at a time, and stop sending several
to that server for the rest of the session. The number of certificates in
the answer says nothing either way: a server that knows the extension may
hold only one of the keys asked for, and a single key ID may match two
certificates.

Also here
---------

An op=get or op=index that matches nothing answers 404, as the HKP draft
asks, with options=mr as without it. It used to answer 200, with a sentence
for op=get and "info:1:0" for op=index.
